#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096672 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096459} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739b1cbc-c1a7-547d-a9fb-1f244bd36827" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640047}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096673 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096460} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd47a43f-1e99-5909-aa48-da8690025197" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639976}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination with Row Exchanges"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097841 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=6 \\\\ y-3z &=-13 \\\\ -x-y-z &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad M = \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -4\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n\n\nWe can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination algorithm. The algorithm that we use is very similar to the case of $2 \\times 2$ systems. \n\nOur goal is to create a descending \"staircase\" of $1$'s with $0$'s below, and we achieve this using elementary row operations. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & -24\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix is now in row echelon form, and we can apply back substitution to solve the original system of equations.\n\n**Note:** The row echelon form of a matrix is *not* unique.  In the previous example, after the second row operation we arrived at\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}{\\color{red}1} & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & -3 & -13 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}-6} & -24\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n\n\nThis augmented matrix is already in row echelon form! However, it is always useful to do an extra step and obtain $1$'s along the main diagonal, like we did previously:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}{\\color{red}1} & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & -3 & -13 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & 4\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n\n\nHaving $1$'s along the main diagonal makes the final back substitution process a little easier." :tutorial/id #uuid "5d8cb1c1-9e09-560f-83ad-7c8a3d0a78cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3155 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097842 :tutorial/content "The **standard Gaussian elimination** for a $3 \\times 3$ system can be described as follows:\n\n1. If $a_{11} \\neq 0$ and $a_{11} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1/a_{11}.$\n\n2. We use first row and the necessary row operations to make the remaining entries of the first column below $a_{11}$ equal to $0.$ Namely,\n\n   - if $a_{21} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-a_{21})R_1;$\n   - if $a_{31} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3 + (-a_{31})R_1.$\n\n3. If $a_{22} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2/a_{22}.$\n\n4. We use the second row and the necessary row operation to make the entry below $a_{22}$ equal to $0.$ Namely,\n\n   - if $a_{32} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3 + (-a_{32})R_2.$\n\n5. If $a_{33} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3/a_{33}.$\n\n6. In some exceptional cases, such as when the element on the diagonal is equal to $0,$ we first need to exchange rows. Then proceed using the same steps as above.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to row echelon form." :tutorial/id #uuid "955c2b9a-3d5f-52c3-b4f3-62541732ffcc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3156 :tutorial/title "General Algorithm"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100875 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100696} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2f5234-df9c-5269-8702-30b4dde9a2ca" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048297}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725532} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719411}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix by the Identity Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100876 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100697} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89362140-1a17-56fd-a9ab-4da336112bee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725533} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186659677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719705}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column or Row of the Identity Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101961 :tutorial/content "Remember that the **identity matrix** $I_n$ is an $n \\times n$ square matrix with $1$'s on the diagonal, and where all other elements are $0.$ For example, the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix $I_3$ is given below:\n\n$$\nI_3 = \n\t\t\t\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe identity matrix gets its name from the fact that whenever we multiply a matrix by the corresponding identity matrix, the matrix remains unchanged.\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nMultiplying the matrix $A$ by the identity matrix $I_3,$ we find that the result is just $A$ itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot {I}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 0 & (-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+10\\cdot 0 & (-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 1 \\\\ 6\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 23\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0+8\\cdot 0 & 23\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 0 & 23\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 0+8\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A \\cdot I_3 = A.$ You can check that $I_3 \\cdot A = A$ using the same method.\n\nThis result holds in general: \n\n> For *any* square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n> \n> \n> $$\n> A \\cdot I_n = I_n \\cdot A = A.\n> $$\n\nIn other words, the $n \\times n$ identity matrix $I_n$ has a similar property to the number $1,$ namely that \n\n$$\na\\cdot 1 = 1\\cdot a = a\n$$\n\n for any real number $a.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5383efb1-6e51-562a-bf79-9ab8cba87ed7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2995 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101962 :tutorial/content "Whenever we multiply a matrix $A$ by the $n$th column of the identity matrix, the result is the $n$th column of $A.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nThe first column of the identity matrix $I_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf we multiply $A$ by the column on the *right*, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1}} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product is just the first column of the matrix $A.$\n\nUsing the same method, you can check for yourself that when we multiply $A$ by the second column of $I_2,$ the result is the second column of $A.$\n\nOn the other hand, if we multiply $A$ by the first *row* of the identity matrix $I_2$ on the *left*, we will obtain the first row of $A$. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}} \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2c46b879-fe8a-5f8e-8579-71b1bc92ac07" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2996 :tutorial/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column of the Identity Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101965 :tutorial/content "We've been using the fact that $AI_n = I_n A = A$ for any $n \\times n$ matrix $A.$ But how do we know for sure?\n\nLet's prove the case of $n=2.$ That is, let's prove that $AI_2=I_2A = A,$ where $A$ is any $2\\times2$ matrix.\n\nAny general $2 \\times 2$ matrix can be written as\n$$\nA =\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nThen, multiplying by $I_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11}\\cdot 1+{a}_{12}\\cdot 0 & {a}_{11}\\cdot 0+{a}_{12}\\cdot 1 \\\\ {a}_{21}\\cdot 1+{a}_{22}\\cdot 0 & {a}_{21}\\cdot 0+{a}_{22}\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above proof shows that the property $A I_2 = I_2 A = A$ holds for all $2\\times2$ matrices $A.$\n\nIn general, to prove that the property $AI_n = I_n A = A$ holds for all $n \\times n$ matrices $A,$ we can use the same method." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c2408f2-4c4d-5bc6-bd0c-e649f5e542f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3132 :tutorial/title "Proving Properties Related to the Identity Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105602} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97cd9132-458b-5234-a3d2-22b633acb74c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713445} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713421}] :knowledge-point/title "Reducing an Augmented Matrix for a Singular 2x2 System to REF and Solving the Resulting System"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105603} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07f2ae4c-ff07-5b99-a7b0-90f460081eef" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714327} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714326}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Singular 2x2 System of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107080 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=-5 \\\\ -3x+6y &=15\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad M = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 15\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 15\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=-5 \\\\ 0x+0y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 2y = -5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we describe the **general solution** of our system.\n\n- The first variable in our equation, $x,$ is called a **basic variable**.\n\n- The second variable in our equation, $y,$ is called a **free variable**.\n\nSolving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x = -5+2y.$ So the general solution of the system is\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-5+2y \\\\ y &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\nwhere the free variable $y$ is any number.\n\nIn vector form, this solution is written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5+2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is the equation of a straight line on a plane. Any point on this line satisfies our system of equations.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $y$ and find the corresponding value of $x$ on the line. For example, if we choose $y=0,$ then the corresponding value is $x=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-5,$ $y=0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0c91df0b-40c7-5a65-9fda-5d2d6438dfa3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3153 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107081 :tutorial/content "Given a $2 \\times 2$ system of linear equations, we have the following possibilities for the number of solutions:\n\n- The system has exactly *one solution* if and only if the respective coefficient matrix is non-singular (meaning that the determinant is not zero). Such systems are called **consistent independent**.\n\n- The system has *infinitely many solutions* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s. These systems are called **consistent dependent**.\n\n- The system has *no solution* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s on the left-hand side and a non-zero element on the right-hand side. These are called **inconsistent** systems.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\n**Note:** A system of linear equations of the form \n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}ax+by &=0 \\\\ cx+dy &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\nwhere we have only $0$'s on the right-hand side, is called **homogeneous**. A homogeneous system always has at least one solution, namely, the solution $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b885f832-9e76-5008-a764-7375b1c25e35" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3154 :tutorial/title "The Number of Solutions of a 2x2 System"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107097 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, a system of equations might not have any solutions. For example, consider the following augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=3 \\\\ 0x+0y &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nThe final equation, $0x+0y=10,$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=10,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "20c281f5-a455-581a-a7f7-fec6cfaa114e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3901 :tutorial/title "Singular Systems with No Solution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115252 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115081} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0303bd06-294e-5f7a-bbd2-b12abdeb93c5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050576}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553585}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 3x3 Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115253 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115082} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41954d0a-b8ce-52a0-a2c0-002b0078a37d" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186661500}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving an Inverse of an Elementary 3x3 Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115254 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115083} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd71abb1-455e-50d0-aae4-082b41821ef1" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186661564}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 3x3 Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116499 :tutorial/content "Just like for $2\\times 2$ matrices, there are also **elementary $3\\times 3$ matrices**. These are matrices that can be obtained by applying *only one* elementary row operation to the identity matrix $I_3 ={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\nFor instance, consider the following $3\\times 3$ matrices:\n\n$$\nE_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\nE_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\nE_3 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThese are all examples of elementary matrices:\n\n- $E_1$ is obtained from $I_3$ by applying the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n- $E_2$ is obtained from $I_3$ by applying the row operation $R_2 := 2R_2.$\n\n- $E_3$ is obtained from $I_3$ by applying the row operation $R_2 := R_2 + 3R_1.$\n\nThey are also examples of the three different types of elementary matrices, defined by the corresponding row operations." :tutorial/id #uuid "4d18540e-8992-5cf5-9984-837579edd2a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3168 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116500 :tutorial/content "As we saw before for elementary $2\\times 2$ matrices, each elementary $3\\times 3$ matrix $E$ is invertible, and the corresponding inverse is always an elementary matrix of the same type as $E.$ To illustrate, let's consider the matrix \n\n$$\nE = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse of $E,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}E$ and the identity matrix $I_3$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n$$\n[{\\color{blue}{E}}\\, |\\, {\\color{black}I_3}]\t\n=\t\n\\left[{\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}{\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}4} & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}0} & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}1} & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}}\\right]\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix. In our case, we need to apply the transformation $R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1.$ We apply the operation to entire row (not only the left part):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,{I}_{3}] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & =[{I}_{3}\\,|\\,{E}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1$ transforms $E$ back into the identity matrix. Likewise, when this row operation is applied to the identity matrix, it generates $E^{-1}.$\n\nThe matrix $E^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n$$\nE^{-1} = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ - 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe can work out the inverse of all elementary $3\\times 3$ matrices in a similar way." :tutorial/id #uuid "90445bd9-d2af-593e-a10d-9e7c94156e49" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3169 :tutorial/title "The Inverse of an Elementary Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119589} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74764043-e1e7-56d6-8f53-9d287a30e538" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570699}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Switching Two Rows"}]}
